WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:04.200
Speaker 1: Let's go next to Grant from Iowa
City.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:05.529
Grant, welcome to the program.

00:00:05.529 --> 00:00:06.529
What's on your mind?

00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:08.019
Speaker 4: Hey, David.

00:00:08.019 --> 00:00:09.019
Great.

00:00:09.019 --> 00:00:10.080
Great to talk with you again.

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.080
Speaker 1: Likewise.

00:00:11.080 --> 00:00:15.250
Speaker 4: So if you would like to have a
variety of different perspectives, I hear

00:00:15.250 --> 00:00:22.769
from in many areas of life just to, like further
develop my own ideas about how I truly feel

00:00:22.769 --> 00:00:23.769
about things.

00:00:23.769 --> 00:00:29.270
And I also think it's good to learn how people
feel about a given topic so that I can empathize

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.150
with them better and connect with them better.

00:00:31.150 --> 00:00:35.390
But I usually don't have an issue doing this
except when it comes to politics.

00:00:35.390 --> 00:00:39.760
I have a hard time finding anyone who's really
interesting or valuable.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:40.809
Listen to on the right.

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:46.480
I was just wondering if you had anyone you
can think of that would be like, come on,

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:53.280
like I guess worth listening to, because I
find usually they're either ignorant or only

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.879
arrogant or even just rude, perhaps.

00:00:55.879 --> 00:01:00.650
And I just it's kind of a waste of time, but
I would like to actually now engage with some

00:01:00.650 --> 00:01:02.449
ideas that I disagree with.

00:01:02.449 --> 00:01:03.449
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:01:03.449 --> 00:01:05.339
Speaker 1: I don't have anybody specific.

00:01:05.339 --> 00:01:06.339
I mean.

00:01:06.339 --> 00:01:10.690
Well, so is there a particular issue on which
you would like such a person?

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.360
Speaker 4: But not really, I guess.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.720
I think just in general.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:17.400
Speaker 1: General right winger.

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:18.400
I don't know.

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.770
I wish I had someone to recommend, but I really
don't at this point in time.

00:01:21.770 --> 00:01:23.030
Speaker 4: Yeah, I wasn't sure if you.

00:01:23.030 --> 00:01:25.110
Well, I know you're busy doing your own thing,
but.

00:01:25.110 --> 00:01:26.110
Speaker 3: Yeah.

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:30.330
Speaker 4: Yeah, it's just because and I have
a fair number of, you know, close relatives

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:34.030
and friends who are conservative, but yeah,
they don't get too involved politically.

00:01:34.030 --> 00:01:37.180
And so it kind of like, well, they don't really
think through the issues too much.

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:42.399
It's kind of just, you know, their gut reaction
to things based on whatever their upbringing

00:01:42.399 --> 00:01:43.399
or their.

00:01:43.399 --> 00:01:44.399
Yeah.

00:01:44.399 --> 00:01:45.399
Theological views or whatever.

00:01:45.399 --> 00:01:46.399
But.

00:01:46.399 --> 00:01:49.719
Speaker 1: And do you like engaging with those
people, or is it more of an annoyance?

00:01:49.719 --> 00:01:52.149
Speaker 4: I enjoy it.

00:01:52.149 --> 00:01:56.670
It's just I feel like we don't really get
down to the nitty gritty of stuff because

00:01:56.670 --> 00:01:59.110
it's pretty surface level.

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:03.990
And the minute I start trying to talk about
something more deeper level, they either get

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:08.500
kind of lost in it or they just lose interest
or yeah, whatever.

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:13.420
I mean, as far as actual political commentators
I've tried listening to, like Ben SHAPIRO

00:02:13.420 --> 00:02:15.470
and it's like it's pretty painful.

00:02:15.470 --> 00:02:16.470
Yeah.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:17.470
Name on the right.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.950
But I just like, can't really do it.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:20.310
Speaker 1: So yeah, no, I don't blame you.

00:02:20.310 --> 00:02:24.910
I mean, you know, what I would suggest is,
rather than just like finding that stuff,

00:02:24.910 --> 00:02:33.250
find some, like, find some books that maybe
would give you a more sensible panorama on

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.540
some of these right wing ideologies from like
more traditional conservatives.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:40.510
I still don't really know exactly who I'd
recommend, but I would go that direction rather

00:02:40.510 --> 00:02:43.310
than trying to figure out, you know, by listening
to Ben SHAPIRO.

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:44.330
Speaker 4: Okay.

00:02:44.330 --> 00:02:45.330
Yeah.

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:46.330
No.

00:02:46.330 --> 00:02:47.330
Speaker 1: All right, my friend.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:48.790
Thank you for the call.

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:49.810
Speaker 4: Okay.

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:50.810
All right.

00:02:50.810 --> 00:02:51.810
Speaker 1: Very good.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:52.849
There is Grant.

00:02:52.849 --> 00:02:54.959
Very, very strong.

00:02:54.959 --> 00:02:58.750
Let's go next to let's go to Elias from Pennsylvania.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:00.500
Elias, welcome to the program.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:01.500
What's on your mind today?

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:03.440
Speaker 3: Hey, David, I haven't called in
a while.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.440
Speaker 1: How are you?

00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:05.680
I'm doing well, thank you.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:10.350
Speaker 3: I have been listening to a whole
bunch of leftist streamers recently, and the

00:03:10.350 --> 00:03:16.830
idea I've been getting is that we need a more
cohesive online left to take advantage of,

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:20.420
you know, the Gen-Z voter base that showed
itself most recently.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.209
And we don't really have a cohesive message.

00:03:22.209 --> 00:03:28.319
And I was wondering if you had any ideas for
gathering the online left to have a more,

00:03:28.319 --> 00:03:30.970
you know, online a stronger online community?

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:36.590
Speaker 1: You know, the reason that this
is really tough is that by definition, the

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:46.099
left is a little more fractured in that we,
I feel, are more resistant to just wanting

00:03:46.099 --> 00:03:54.680
to be part of, like, one big tent to some
degree because.

00:03:54.680 --> 00:03:59.420
We often allow the perfect to become the enemy
of the good.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:04.540
And what I don't like sometimes seeing on
the left is people who are aligned on 90%

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:10.019
of issues saying, I can't be with this person
or that person in the same coalition because

00:04:10.019 --> 00:04:12.450
of the five or 10% on which we differ.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:16.910
And I think that that fundamentally gets in
the way of building the bigger consensus like

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:18.100
you're talking about.

00:04:18.100 --> 00:04:20.630
I would love to see that to a great degree.

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:22.479
I don't know who would organize it.

00:04:22.479 --> 00:04:27.770
And I think that there's something about the
left that is more antithetical to welcoming

00:04:27.770 --> 00:04:29.150
and accepting that.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:30.980
So I'd have to devote some more thought to
it.

00:04:30.980 --> 00:04:31.980
I love the idea.

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:33.720
I think the left needs to be better organized.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:38.980
I just don't know exactly what the hub or
the crux of it would be.

00:04:38.980 --> 00:04:41.660
Hello.

00:04:41.660 --> 00:04:44.330
Hello.

00:04:44.330 --> 00:04:47.000
Well.

00:04:47.000 --> 00:04:48.000
I guess he's gone.

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.000
Okay, that was.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:49.039
That was weird

